{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggY5VBNDUcitrCwGugA2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Answer: Dq ≈ 1070 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 862 cm⁻¹.** C cannot be determined from these bands.\n\n**Assigning the bands.** A high-spin octahedral d⁷ ion has a ⁴T₁g(F) ground state, so it shows three spin-allowed bands:\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₂g = 9 460 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴A₂g = 20 160 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₁g(P) = 21 160 cm⁻¹\n\n**Getting Dq.** The gap between ⁴A₂g and ⁴T₂g is exactly 10Dq. So 10Dq = 20 160 − 9 460 = 10 700 cm⁻¹, which gives Dq ≈ 1070 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Checking the other assignment.** If the two upper bands are swapped (⁴A₂g at 21 160 cm⁻¹), then Dq = 1170 cm⁻¹. Fitting ν₁ with that value needs B ≈ 14 000 cm⁻¹, which is not physical. So ⁴A₂g is the 20 160 cm⁻¹ band.\n\n**Getting B.** The two ⁴T₁g states mix, so their energies are given by:\n- ν₁ = 5Dq − 7.5B + ½S\n- ν₂ = 15Dq − 7.5B + ½S\n- ν₃ = S\n- where S = √(225B² + 100Dq² + 180B·Dq)\n\nI fitted all three bands at once by least squares. The result is Dq = 1069.7 cm⁻¹ and B = 862.3 cm⁻¹.\n\n**How good the fit is.**\n\n| Band | Observed (cm⁻¹) | Calculated (cm⁻¹) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ν₁ | 9 460 | 9 462 |\n| ν₂ | 20 160 | 20 159 |\n| ν₃ | 21 160 | 21 160 |\n\nThe root-mean-square error is about 1 cm⁻¹. B is about 77% of the Co²⁺ free-ion value (~1120 cm⁻¹), which suggests a moderately covalent metal–ligand bond. Dq/B ≈ 1.24, well on the high-spin side of the spin crossover.\n\n**Why C is left out.** The fit used a default C of 4499 cm⁻¹, but a 5% change in C does not move the spin-allowed bands. So C is not determined by these data.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1070, \"B\": 862, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"High-spin d7 assignment 4T2, 4A2, 4T1(P) gives 10Dq = ν2 − ν1 and a three-band fit gives B ≈ 862 cm^-1; C is not determined.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_c